foreign  [Music]  you will learn how to troubleshoot and  resolve throttling jobs in data proc on  compute engine clusters to begin let's  look at what a data product job is it is  a program or a script you want to run on  a dataproc cluster typically to perform  processing of data you can submit a job  using Google Cloud console Google Cloud  CLI API or login directly into the  primary virtual machine or VM through  SSH keep in mind a dataproc job is not  same as a yarn application rather a job  can be made up of one or more yarn  applications when a job is stuck in  running State and lacking the resources  it needs for Progress the master VM  throttles or delays the job before  diving into troubleshooting a throttle  job let's understand the life of a data  proc job as soon as a job is submitted  it gets into the pending state it  remains in the state until the data  agent acquires it if the dataproc agent  acquires the job then it gets into the  running State sometimes a job may not be  acquired due to failure in the agent  compute engine Network or some other  calls then the state of the job is set  as error once the job is in running  State the data proc agent then checks  for resources to make sure the job  driver can process it this is a crucial  step since the resources can vary on  many parameters like concurrent running  jobs memory CPU consumption size of  cluster Etc if the resources are not  available the job is throttled or  delayed and its state is set as skewed  otherwise the job spins up drivers and  applications progresses and finally  exits upon completion let's now get into  action and learn how to troubleshoot and  resolve a data product job that took  seven times longer to finish than normal  I'll start with Google Google Cloud  console but it doesn't give any other  clue or information on what caused this  delay so I'll check the status of the  job in Google Cloud CLI by running the  gcloud dataproc jobs describe command  check the status history it shows that  the job was in running state for longer  than expected recall from the discussion  on life of a data proc job that if a job  is in running state it means that the  dataproc agent had acquired the job and  moved it to the running State however  the job could have been throttled due to  lack of resources to investigate this  issue further let's check if there was a  high resource consumption when the job  was running go to Cloud monitoring and  use the filter to view the CPU  utilization for the duration when the  job was running well it all looks good  next step is to analyze the dataproc  agent logs as it contains the progress  logs of the jobs let's go to the log  Explorer page and use this filter to  view the errors and warnings for  specific job ID and cluster set the time  interval to the duration in which the  job is running  there you go the cluster complains of  being throttled for too many jobs  expand the details to drill down the  metadata  ah it says maximum one job allowed which  seems to be the cause for this issue  this means that only one job can be run  at a time since there are many jobs  running simultaneously the job will be  throttled and hence the delay in  completion to validate this let's check  the cluster configuration this can be  done by capturing the Diagnostics file  using the following diagnose command  remember this file should be collected  while the cluster still exists  navigate to the conf folder and open the  data proc  dataproc.properties file indeed dataproc  Max concurrent jobs is set to 1. this is  the cause of the issue to resolve the  issue increase the max concurrent jobs  by setting a higher value to  scheduler.max concurrent jobs property  remember if this value is not set when  the cluster is created the upper limit  on concurrent jobs is calculated using  this formula Master memory MB is  determined by the primary vm's machine  type mesh memory mb per job is 1024 by  default and is configurable at cluster  Creation with scheduler driver size MB  property alternatively you can set the  property scheduler driver size MB to a  higher value this property determines  the maximum number of concurrent jobs a  cluster will run keep in mind if both  Max concrete jobs and driver-sized  memory are configured the max concurrent  jobs takes precedent consider upgrading  your cluster to a bigger machine size if  more capacity is required it is helpful  to note that a dataproc job can get  throttled if the primary agent fails to  acquire the resources due to any of the  additional reasons other issues like  increased data volume dependency  resolution can also make your data  product job throttled the tools that we  covered in this video should be a good  start for your investigation stay tuned  for more videos to troubleshoot failed  jobs on dataproc for more information  about troubleshooting throttle jobs  check out the following documentation  thanks for watching foreign  [Music]

